the german government has said it is  giving more than 1 billion euros in  military aid to ukraine the funds are  part of a 2 billion increase in defense  spending announced on friday over half  of that money will go to the european  peace facility an organization which  purchases weapons for ukraine the move  follows heavy criticism of german  chancellor olaf schulz for his hesitancy  to send heavy weapons to ukraine  the military was able to repel russian  forces at the gates of kiev but as dw's  alexandre von naaman reports soldiers  say they had to use weapons made in the  last century  on the move with members of the 114th  brigade part of ukraine's territorial  defense force  we are in metrevka one of the villages  that were in the way of the russian  invaders and their offensive on kiev  convoy was approaching on the street  we were holding our positions at the  neighbouring village and the highway  they came from the left we were told to  reposition to strengthen our line then  the squadron's commander gave the order  to open  fire lawyer that's his nickname shows me  drone footage of the attack the russians  were disoriented he says they didn't  seem to expect resistance they tried to  break through but lawyer with his  soviet-made rocket launcher stood in  their way  if we'd had modern weapons we would have  destroyed them all  but we had to use what we've got  weapons from the last  the  century  forces do not lack enthusiasm or the  courage to fight against all odds but  what they say they need are heavy  weapons as soon as possible as the whole  country braces for a great battle in the  east only better equipped and armed they  say they can repel russian attacks and  prevent more war crimes from happening  many fear the destruction left behind by  russian soldiers around kiev is a grim  omen of what may be in store for the  east  cook is originally from the donetsk  region in eastern ukraine  i have mixed feelings it's hard you feel  pain grief but we are soldiers so we  have to pull ourselves together we must  not show our pain and fear  our children and wives depend on us  and they depend on each other the worst  moment is the one before the battle cook  says but when it starts he adds the fear  is gone  let's bring in dw political  correspondent benjamin alvarez gruber  who is at one of germany's so-called  easter marches for peace here in berlin  benjamin  chancellor schultz is now making this  big  financial concession will this be enough  to silence critics  many say it's important step in the  direction of further supporting ukraine  financially and also what finance  minister christian lindner pledged with  this additional help saying that most of  it will go to ukraine but many critics  will demand two things or will continue  demanding two things on one side the  embargo when it comes to oil and gas  from russia or  sending heavy weapons to ukraine that  germany has not pledged yet  so why is that i mean as you just  mentioned ukraines have repeatedly asked  for heavy weaponry a heavy military  equipment but germany seems to be happy  to pay for weapons but not deliver them  why not  that's right the criticism is mounting  also within the government there was a  delegation that met with ukrainian  officials saying that would what ukraine  needs now are heavy weapons and even  foreign minister analina during a recent  meeting in luxembourg also said that  heavy weapons are needed also implying  that germany should also  also give them but  german chancellor olaf scholz  has said that germany has supported  world support and also will further  support um  ukraine but has not specifically  mentioned  weapons defense minister christina  lambrecht said that that has one reason  that the enemy is also listening and  that if germany is going to supply these  weapons they will not talk about it  now uh and of course  then there is also pressure mounting uh  from the other direction i mean you are  at one of germany's eastern marches uh  it's a political pacifist protest that's  going back to the 1960s anti-nuclear  protests and so forth  do you get the sense that this war in  ukraine is having any impact on this  movement and and how war has been  perceived  it definitely has a huge impact you just  mentioned this was a movement that was  really big in the 1960s 1980s calling  for disarmament especially also nuclear  disarmament but that has changed and  that has  shifted but when we look at these  protests right behind me there's been  criticism because they say that one of  the reasons that this war occurred is  also due to the nato expansion so for  them this  the stance of being peaceful and not  sending weapons and being against the  special fund  for the bundesville has earned criticism  not only by organizers that are  organizing an alternative peace protest  here in berlin with syrian and also with  ukrainian activists calling for weapons  saying that you can also be a pacifist  and call for the end while also  supplying and pledging to supply these  weapons for ukraine and this criticism  has also been from within the government  we had vice chancellor robert habek  saying that these protests should be the  criticism of this protest should be  pointed towards vladimir putin and the  war so we're seeing an incredible a very  interesting shift in german politics not  only when it comes to supplying this  weapon but also on this discourse on  this discussion what it means to be a  pacifist and if just standing by from  the sidelines and seeing how russia  continues to attack ukraine will be will  be enough  to get a peace again with many saying  that if you actually want peace and you  actually want to support ukraine they  need more than just solidarity on social  media they need either this oil and gas  embargo that i mentioned earlier or  delivery of heavy weapons that ukrainian  president vladom volodomir celensky has  repeatedly asked for all right tw  political correspondent benjamin alvarez  gruber there reporting from one of  germany's easter marches a pacifist  political movement in times of a war  with ukraine thank you so much  you
 

